! Model tiers, 2026 ~ each tier where its nature serves the whole

A read-only scout measured 115 Claude subagent transcripts (2026-10-17 to 2026-10-30), the day's Codex seats, the span's merged PRs and the lane branches, then collided the measures with the providers' pages and published orchestration studies. Marks: ''SOURCED'' cites a page and the date fetched; ''MEASURED'' names the script or field read from this machine (transcript `message.model` and `usage` deduplicated per message id, `spend.py`, seat `final.md`, `git log` committer time); ''REASONED'' marks judgment; ''UNVERIFIED'' marks an aggregator's claim this crew traced to no primary page. "API-eq $" prices tokens at public API rates to weigh seats against each other; neither plan meters in those units. One lane-day stands behind each seat, so every lean here runs LEAN-verify.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-field>>

!! What the providers say

Anthropic places Opus 5.5 first: "start with Claude Opus 5.5 for most workloads. Use Claude Fable 5.1 for demanding reasoning and long-horizon agentic work, or when your evals on Claude Opus 5.5 at higher effort still fall short" (SOURCED, https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/overview, 2026-10-29). Fable 5.1 bills $10 / $50 with cache hits at 0.025x input ($0.25), against Opus 5.5 at $4 / $20 with hits at 0.05x ($0.20) and Sonnet 5.5 at $2 / $10 with hits at $0.10 (SOURCED, https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing, 2026-10-29): a seat that mostly re-reads its cache pays about 1.25x Opus on Fable, a seat that writes and speaks pays 2.5x. Max carries "a separate weekly limit for Fable" beside the shared limits (SOURCED, https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11145838, 2026-10-29). Aggregators copy a Fable 5.1 launch table against Opus 5, never Opus 5.5 (UNVERIFIED); no public number shows Fable 5.1 above Opus 5.5 on agentic coding (REASONED).

OpenAI points complex builds at Astra and then Sol 6.1 ("Near-Astra performance for complex work at a lower cost"), positions Terra for "clear writing, visual understanding, and computer use", and Luna for "focused, high-volume tasks, including summarization, extraction, and focused coding", started at High (SOURCED, https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/models, 2026-10-29). Prices per million, input / cached / output: Sol 6.1 2 / 0.10 / 10; Terra 6.1 1 / 0.05 / 5; Luna 0.10 / 0.01 / 0.50 (SOURCED, https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing, 2026-10-29).

Orchestration studies: an Opus lead over Sonnet subagents beat a single Opus by 90.2% on research, token use explained 80% of variance, and "most coding tasks involve fewer truly parallelizable tasks than research" (SOURCED, https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system, 2026-10-29). An Opus advisor consulted by a Sonnet executor raised SWE-bench Multilingual 2.7 points at 11.9% lower cost, the advisor speaking 400 to 700 tokens (SOURCED, https://claude.com/blog/the-advisor-strategy, 2026-10-29). Across 180 configurations, multi-agent variants lowered strictly sequential planning by 39 to 70% and gained up to about 81% on parallel work under central coordination (SOURCED, https://research.google/blog/towards-a-science-of-scaling-agent-systems-when-and-why-agent-systems-work/, 2026-10-29).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-this-crew-measured>>

!! What this crew measured

''Narration stays small at every tier.'' Visible text makes under 3% of output tokens in every group; hidden adaptive thinking makes about 58 to 69% and tool inputs about 30 to 35% (MEASURED, chars/4 estimate against `usage.output_tokens`). Fable leads speak 22 text characters per tool call against Opus 10 and Sonnet 3, yet that text costs 0.8% of their output. Fable batches: 1.52 tool calls per API call as lead and 6.48 as a web scout, against about 1.0 for Opus and Sonnet (MEASURED).

''The one-hour subagent cache works.'' Sonnet lead cache write:read fell from 0.079 to 0.010 and writes fell from 64% to 22% of Sonnet lead cost (MEASURED; E1 of `model-roster-2026` reads PASS on the API meter, the plan meter unread). Every large cache rewrite followed an idle gap over one hour; idle rewrites made about a third of three standing Fable leads' bill (MEASURED).

''Fable paid in bursts, not in waits.'' Two standing Fable leads (17 to 19 hours) ran $6.2 and $8.6 API-eq per commit; a fresh Fable lead from a cold brief ran 1.6 hours, 19 commits, 8 reds, $2.1 per commit, no rewrite, and its live stand measured four faults and cured three (MEASURED). An Opus lead ran $2.4, a Sonnet lead on a listable lane $1.1, Sonnet leads on hygiene lanes $0.9 per commit; the Fable lanes held the most integration-dense rows, so cost per commit flatters the listed lanes (REASONED). The same tokens priced at Opus rates cost 0.50 to 0.57 of their Fable bill (MEASURED).

''Terra yields on lists.'' Twenty-one Terra build seats: 11 full, 5 partial, 5 empty, about $2.5 API-eq for the day; 6 of 6 full on listed census slices, 0 of 3 on integration slices (MEASURED, seat `final.md`). Yield tracks the slice, not the lead's tier (REASONED).

''Reads carry the truth cheapest.'' Five of five finished Sol 6.1 high reads carried a HIGH or BLOCKER the lead acted on, about $1 API-eq each; among them a steward fault that poisoned a canon carrier. Ten Luna high reads cost about $0.18 in all and carried a blocker, a HIGH, and the MED that led to the second real SharedWorker race (MEASURED, seat `final.md` and `controller/plan.md`).

''The controller re-reads itself.'' The Opus main session ran about $76 API-eq on the day, 18% of Claude spend, 79% of it cache reads of its own long context (MEASURED).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-placement>>
!! The placement ~ what each seat takes, in order

# Seat the controller on Opus 5.5 at high effort; refresh its context at each batch land. Take Fable only for a named ruling Opus at max visibly missed.
# Lead a listable lane with a Sonnet 5.5 subagent on the one-hour cache. Move to Opus for a seam, custody or security lane.
# Summon a Fable 5.1 lead as a burst, only when all four hold: a named integration-judgment bottleneck; the lead authors the code; one lane-day at most with no wait past an hour (a brief, then a fresh seat); the weekly Fable pool holds room.
# Build with Codex `gpt-6.1-terra` at medium on file-listed edits. Author judgment slices at the lead. Weigh a `gpt-6.1-sol` build seat once E2 reads. Seat no Opus or Fable builder.
# Read every batch with Codex `gpt-6-luna` at high. Move to a fresh Claude reader where the read needs house canon.
# Read every batch head, and every caps, custody, crypto, gate or Keyhive slice, with Codex `gpt-6.1-sol` at high; a fresh Opus subagent serves where Sol cannot.
# Cold-read a spec with open forks that an expensive builder will inherit with Fable 5.1; take Opus otherwise.
# Research with Fable for parallel web collides, Opus for repo-grounded questions, Codex Sol with `--search` for one question; Sonnet serves audits and sweeps.

A walled Codex seat writes and stops; the lead commits at each red and fix boundary. A Codex seat beats a Claude helper on every read, every listed build and one-question research; a Claude helper wins where the work needs house canon, SendMessage continuity, a commit, the battery lock, or a browser.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/stories>>

!! Two stories

''Gaia-2026.'' A clinic co-op's household Nexus owes passkey recovery before the rainy season. A Sonnet lead, Terra on the listed files, Luna on every slice and Sol on the custody batch carry it. A nurse's face then fails to seat across two clinics where three processes disagree on the realm steward; Terra returns inspection-only twice. One fresh Fable lead takes the knot by its own hand in one sitting and retires into a brief once the walk stands live. The co-op pays the Fable price once, for the knot.

''Elyncia-5492.'' A wright-guild seats a great-wright over a whole season's re-binding; he waits by the press for apprentices who return blank pages and re-reads the archive after every wait. By spring the guild rules: summon the great-wright for a knot, let him cut it by hand and leave a brief; the guild-master stays the guild-master; the slow lantern-reader reads every binding, since she caught a poisoned folio at each one. Whether her yield holds once the easy faults are gone stays for E3.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open>>

!! Open

# Whether a Sol 6.1 read at every batch head fits the Codex pool: about 0.6% of the week per read, about 13% a week at three batches a day.
# Whether the Max weekly percent per lane-day fell with the one-hour cache (E1's plan-meter half).
# Whether a Sol 6.1 build seat lands judgment slices that Terra leaves empty (E2).
# Whether Sol's high-finding yield holds across a second lane-day (E3).

